{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 4,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.046,
            0.347,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.088,
            0.848,
            0.127
          ],
          "content": "The expression 4m + 2q will always evaluate to an even number for any integer values of m and q. We can justify this in two different ways —"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.131,
            0.846,
            0.169
          ],
          "content": "We know 4m is even and 2q is even for any integers m and q. Therefore, their sum will also be even."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.173,
            0.848,
            0.249
          ],
          "content": "The expression $4 m \\pm 2 q$ is equal to the expression $2 ( 2 m + q )$ . Here, the expression $2 ( 2 m + q )$ means 2 times $2 m + q .$ In other words, 2 is a factor of this expression. Therefore, this expression will always give an even number for any integers m and q."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.253,
            0.847,
            0.29
          ],
          "content": "For example, if m = 4 and $q = - 9 ,$ , the expression 4m + 2q becomes $4 \\times 4 + 2 \\times ( - 9) = - 2$ , which is an even number."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.289,
            0.848,
            0.364
          ],
          "content": "In the expression x<sup>2</sup> + 2, x<sup>2</sup> is even if x is even, and $x ^ { 2 }$ is odd if x is odd. Therefore, the expression $x ^ { 2 }   +   2$ will **not** always give an even number. An example and a non-example for when the expression evaluates to an even number — (i) if x = 6, then $x ^ { 2 } + 2 = 3 8$ , and (ii) if x = 3, then $x^{2} + 2 = 11.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.371,
            0.848,
            0.431
          ],
          "content": "？ Similarly, determine and explain which of the other expressions always give even numbers. Write a couple of examples and non-examples, as appropriate, for each expression."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.444,
            0.836,
            0.467
          ],
          "content": "？ Write a few algebraic expressions which always give an even number."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.486,
            0.395,
            0.507
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Pairs to Make Fours"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.523,
            0.791,
            0.545
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Take a pair of even numbers. Add them. Is the sum divisible by 4?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.547,
            0.606,
            0.566
          ],
          "content": "Try this with different pairs of even numbers."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.566,
            0.658,
            0.585
          ],
          "content": "When is the sum a multiple of 4, and when is it not?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.585,
            0.503,
            0.603
          ],
          "content": "Is there a general rule or a pattern?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.602,
            0.847,
            0.641
          ],
          "content": "Even numbers can be of two types based on the remainders they leave when divided by 4."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.228,
            0.674,
            0.393,
            0.798
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
